#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049032 :topic/id #uuid "d4ff589f-1af0-5477-b78d-91f6dfb7f322" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198362 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198236 :question/id #uuid "6d2cd5bf-899f-5b81-9512-ee98489d17ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-7322" :question/problem "Consider the region $D,$ given by\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 2 \\leq \\sqrt{xy} \\leq 4, \\: 2x \\leq y \\leq 3x \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere $u=\\dfrac{y}{x}$ and $v=xy.$ Find the representation of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)$ is the image of $D$ under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nLet's find the image of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane. To do that, we write the region $D$ in terms of $u$ and $v,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(D) & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le \\sqrt{xy}\\le 4,\\,2x\\le y\\le 3x\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,{2}^{2}\\le (\\sqrt{xy})^{2}\\le {4}^{2},\\,2\\le \\frac{y}{x}\\le 3\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,4\\le xy\\le 16,\\,2\\le \\frac{y}{x}\\le 3\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,4\\le v\\le 16,\\,2\\le u\\le 3\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,4\\le v\\le 16\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $2 \\leq \\sqrt{xy}\\leq 4,$ we must have $x \\neq 0.$ Therefore, $u=\\dfrac{y}{x}$ is well-defined."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6d98056-016e-520b-b752-d6a529f2c02d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Rectangular Preimage Given a Transformation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198363 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198237 :question/id #uuid "c78f087b-f0e5-58e6-a7a1-92a78c85c4d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-7323" :question/problem "Let $D$ be the finite region bounded by the curve \n$(x+y)^2 + y = 3$ and the line $y=-1.$\nConsider the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere $u=x+y$ and $v=y.$ Find the representation of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we are given that  \n\n![](images/0d/0da1f765ddb2f8e38296bb80b4e44e1f1489bad06273ee639df1dadca43c35e5.svg)\n\nSubstituting $x+y = u$ and $y = v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{2}+y=3\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}+v=3\\,⟹\\,v=3-{u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y = v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-1\\,⟹\\,v=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=3-{u}^{2} \\\\ v &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=\\pm 2 \\\\ v &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2, -1).$\n\n![](images/84/84c624aab456b9a090c9c4e6d23c82d8e1f69425050b6f3ee9d4afd18e3f0cec.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq u \\leq 2, \\quad  -1 \\leq v \\leq 3-u^2 \\big\\}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9156e571-2fe9-5347-95da-c1c97de1a3c9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Transformation That Maps a Region Onto a Type I Region"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198376 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198018 :question/id #uuid "87c2cf96-8772-5b28-b6d8-a5616b38543f" :question/math-academy-id "e-12337" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ enclosed in the first quadrant of the $xy$-plane between the curves\n\n$$\ny=x, \\qquad\ny=7x, \\qquad\nx=\\dfrac{3}{y^2}, \\qquad\nx=\\dfrac{5}{y^2}.\n$$\n\nA transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nmaps a rectangular region $\\Delta$ with sides parallel to the $u$- and $v$-axes from the $uv$-plane onto $D$ in the $xy$-plane. Given that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T} : (u,v) \\to \\Big(\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{v}{u^2}}, \\: \\bbox[5pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} \\, \\Big),\n$$\n\nwhat could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "We would like to find a rectangular region of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v\\le d\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u(x,y)\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v(x,y)\\le d\\},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the equations of the left, right, bottom, and top sides are\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}u(x,y)} = a, \\qquad \n{\\color{blue}u(x,y)} = b, \\qquad\n{\\color{red}v(x,y)} = c, \\qquad \n{\\color{red}v(x,y)} = d.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze the boundary of our region $D.$ \n\nRewriting the equations \n\n$$\ny=x, \\qquad\ny=7x, \\qquad\nx=\\dfrac{3}{y^2}, \\qquad\nx=\\dfrac{5}{y^2}\n$$\n\nby moving both variables $x$ and $y$ to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x}=1,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=7,\\,x{y}^{2}=3,\\,x{y}^{2}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we put $u(x,y)={\\color{blue}\\dfrac{y}{x}}$ and $v(x,y)={\\color{red}xy^2},$ we can define the transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1} : (x,y) \\to \\left( \\, {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{y}{x}}\\,, \\: \\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{\\color{red}xy^2} \\right).\n$$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{T},$ we should express $x$ and $y$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$ Recall that we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ v &=x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, $y=ux.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get \n\n$$\nv = u^2x^3\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx^3 = \\dfrac{v}{u^2} \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx = \\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{v}{u^2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x = \\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{v}{u^2}}$ back into $y=ux,$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny =u\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{v}{u^2}} = \\sqrt[3]{uv}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ can be given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T} : (u,v) \\to \\left(\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{v}{u^2}}, \\, \\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{ \\sqrt[3]{uv}} \\right).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de3fa2fa-304c-583a-8c73-a5ff1db972e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Transformation That Maps a Rectangular Region Onto a Given One"}] :topic/math-academy-id 4134 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050705}] :topic/title "Transformations of Regions Between Curves"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049997 :topic/difficulty 1.1044540289337517 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428676 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1/2 (1/2); multivariable=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 2151->1930. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048942} :encompassing/weight 0.5} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428677 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 2151->1992. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050207} :encompassing/weight 0.5} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428678 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/multivariable-frequency-estimates.json, pair 2151->2000. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050546} :encompassing/weight 0.5}] :topic/id #uuid "24e61144-3626-5d23-b2e6-a33ee94f32f3" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133833 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133648 :question/id #uuid "51467bae-f181-53ba-adf2-1218dc16275f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7024" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the lines $x=0,$ $y=0,$ and the curve $y=\\sqrt{4 - x^2}.$ What is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D (x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A$ expressed as a repeated integral?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/3d/3d67d73d3ede011d6c2fe4bc9e160aa3c2af522bff6cddfcc7c351c97418e764.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x  \\leq 2, \\quad 0 \\leq y \\leq \\sqrt{4-x^2} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}(x+y)\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e891363-fea2-5de4-9bbb-67fddb077200" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Given"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133834 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133649 :question/id #uuid "7f443df5-1c26-5af4-9541-e83522126197" :question/math-academy-id "e-7025" :question/problem "If $D$ is the finite region in the first quadrant enclosed by the curves $y=3x$ and $y=x^3,$ then what is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} f\\left( {x,y} \\right) \\, \\mathrm{d}A$ expressed as a repeated integral?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/f8/f8a5fd7fc3e98d7f08b5ea48636e5de4a1a45b7a94fbe9e968789afcfe3a582c.png)\n\nNow, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & =3x \\\\ {x}^{3}-3x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are $0 \\le x \\le \\sqrt{3}.$ Note that we disregarded the solution $x=-\\sqrt 3$ since $x\\geq 0$ in our region.\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/23/232f885d2230694bf62f81f5987cd2b3a12a49430d90d2cae4712c552587a48c.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x  \\leq \\sqrt{3}, \\quad x^3 \\leq y \\leq 3x \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{3x}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}[{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{3x}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "853b2770-5057-50aa-9c15-7426cf4cc6a4" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Not Given"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133835 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133650 :question/id #uuid "b151be22-91a1-5303-b86d-4f9ba748f000" :question/math-academy-id "e-7026" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 \\int_0^x x^2y \\,\\textrm{d}y \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\,dx & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{5}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a28c4320-e2dd-5195-81ac-b910c2328691" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral Defined Over a Type I Region"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133836 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133651 :question/id #uuid "24dd94c8-2821-5614-9ccf-43c3656267f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-7027" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint \\limits_{D} xy\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\:$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region below the $x$-axis enclosed by the circle $x^2 + y^2 = 4.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $y \\leq 0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 = 4\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\ny = -\\sqrt{4-x^2}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/31/31e7bb281a36a3676730de0883d7abd5445093713de1bbd41899cce50a7e0e48.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- We already know the intersection of the circle with the line $y=0.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$-2 \\le x \\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/2a/2a84d17b83fb1e2036c6edc0a71c5c10a58bf893c33bbf38662ebb417aab3164.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD = \\Big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\quad -\\sqrt{4-x^2} \\leq y \\leq 0 \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iint \\limits_{D} xy\\,\\mathrm{d}A = \\int_{-2}^2 \\int_{-\\sqrt{4-x^2}}^{0} xy\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\: \\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}^{0}xy\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}x[{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}^{0}y\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}^{0}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}x(0-(-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}})^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}x(-(4-{x}^{2}))\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}x({x}^{2}-4)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({x}^{3}-4x)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({x}^{3}-4x)\\,dx & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-2{x}^{2}]}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((4-8)-(4-8)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69f6b2a7-7a3a-5c2c-90db-5aa321784048" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type I Region"}] :topic/math-academy-id 2151 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050705}] :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Type I Regions"}]}
